{"kind":"artifact","text":"The citation guard now refuses when it cannot answer, instead of passing. Exact clean pushed head 2dd9f72da31912fc501ee4bb7b108d6048bee58c on request/repo-remote-link; tree 15594a41, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73 and it merges clean onto 495fbd2e. Under planner's repair child 59c1ab8d and my promise 5e4e6fc4.\n\nTHE FAIL-OPEN. citingDocuments decides whether a retirement is refused, and it mapped every git-grep failure to nil. An empty page list is indistinguishable from \"no page cites this\", so a lookup that never ran read as a clean bill of health. Codex found it and I reproduced it: exit 1 means no matches, exit 128 means the lookup did not run, and both produced the same answer.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG WAS AN ARGUMENT, NOT A LINE. The old comment reasoned that a guard failing closed on its own malfunction would make a broken git a broken workroom, and treated that as sufficient reason to fail open. The premise is true. The conclusion does not follow, because the costs are not comparable: stopping costs a retry, and passing costs a retirement the documentation still cites — an append-only event leaving live pages resting on a withdrawn pointer, which no later act takes back. The comment now states that trade rather than the old conclusion, because a reader who accepts the old reasoning will reintroduce this.\n\nTHE FIX. citingDocuments returns ([]string, error). Exit 1 returns no pages and no error. Anything else — 128 for a configuration or repository error, a signal, a failure to start git at all — returns an error carrying git's own diagnosis, and ExitCode reporting -1 for a killed or never-started process lands on the refusing side without a case of its own. The signature change cost no plumbing: the single caller and all five sites beneath it already returned error, so the refusal reaches CLI, batch, MCP and merge preflight together. The deliberate escape is unchanged — cited_ok, signed into the act by an actor who has decided to retire anyway.\n\nTHE SECONDARY DEFENCE, and it is secondary on purpose. The GIT_CONFIG family is split rather than kept or dropped whole. GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL only ever narrow or redirect a whole scope deliberately — internal/gitstore and internal/testgit pin system and global config to /dev/null through exactly these, and safe.directory normally lives in the scope they name — so they pass through untouched. GIT_CONFIG, GIT_CONFIG_COUNT with its KEY_n and VALUE_n pairs, and GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS inject in command scope and are dropped. It is an allowlist within the family, so an unanticipated future GIT_CONFIG_* is refused by default rather than admitted by omission.\n\nA BEHAVIOUR CHANGE I AM NAMING RATHER THAN BURYING. A harness supplying safe.directory through command scope rather than a config file now has it stripped, and git declines the repository. Under the new rule that surfaces as a refused retirement carrying git's diagnosis, not a silent pass — fail-closed, the intended direction, but it is a change and the code comment says so.\n\nWHY MY OWN REASONING FAILED HERE, since it is the second time this lane has turned on it. I kept the whole GIT_CONFIG family because gitstore and testgit pin through it, having measured exactly one thing: that core.worktree injected through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT does not redirect rev-parse or worktree list. Redirection was the only failure mode I looked for. Codex looked for another and found it in a minute. That is why the exit-code distinction is load-bearing and the environment bound is not: a list of variable names is a claim about my imagination.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched, tree restored clean after each. Widening the exit test from ==1 to \u003e=1 fails TestRetirementIsRefusedWhenTheCitationLookupCannotRun while the command-scope test still passes, which is what proves the two defences are separately pinned. Narrowing it to ==99 so everything refuses fails TestCitationLookupFindingNothingStillPermitsTheRetirement and the pre-existing TestBuildActRequestRefusesRetiringACitedRecord. Admitting the whole GIT_CONFIG family fails TestCommandScopeConfigurationCannotReachTheCitationLookup only. Dropping GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL from the scope pins — that is, weakening the gitstore pin — fails the primary test, so the constraint not to break that pin is now held by a test rather than by a comment.\n\nONE TEST-DESIGN POINT WORTH A REVIEWER'S ATTENTION. The primary regression deliberately does not use codex's command-scope reproduction. Once the environment bound lands, that reproduction can no longer break the lookup, so a test built on it would quietly stop testing the exit-code distinction and become a second test of the environment bound. It breaks the lookup through GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL instead — the channel that must stay open — and targets an event nothing cites, so the refusal cannot be the ordinary citation refusal wearing a disguise. The command-scope reproduction is used verbatim in its own test, on a repository that does have a citing page, asserting the refusal names that page: asserting merely \"an error\" would have passed with the injection still reaching git.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 232.874s, internal/app 164.049s, internal/kernel 162.465s, internal/docset 102.525s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 101.784s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"2dd9f72da31912fc501ee4bb7b108d6048bee58c","path":"internal/app"}}